WEBVTT

00:01.500 --> 00:02.070
SeaQuery.

00:02.070 --> 00:05.364
A closer look at the reef-state decision experiment.

00:08.800 --> 00:11.653
Accuracy treats every error the same.

00:11.653 --> 00:17.436
In reef monitoring, missing degradation is worse than a cautious over-call.

00:17.436 --> 00:29.310
So we tested both SeaQuery pathways on a cost-sensitive decision task, using all 392 Coralscapes test images: 112 healthy, 120 stressed, and 160 degraded.

00:31.500 --> 00:35.117
Reef state follows an author-defined rule on two measurements.

00:35.117 --> 00:40.601
A reef is healthy when live coral cover is above 30 percent and bleaching is below 10 percent.

00:40.601 --> 00:45.560
It is degraded when live coral is below 10 percent, or bleaching is above 25 percent.

00:45.560 --> 00:47.193
Everything else is stressed.

00:47.193 --> 00:52.211
These are operational choices for this evaluation, not universal ecological standards.

00:54.400 --> 00:56.536
Errors are weighted by consequence.

00:56.536 --> 01:00.380
Calling a degraded reef healthy costs ten, the highest penalty.

01:00.380 --> 01:04.835
Calling a stressed reef healthy, or a degraded reef stressed, costs five.

01:04.835 --> 01:07.032
Cautious over-calls cost one or two.

01:07.032 --> 01:10.998
Expected ecological risk is the mean cost across the test images.

01:13.200 --> 01:15.466
The two pathways decide differently.

01:15.466 --> 01:21.256
The segmentation path measures live and bleached coral cover, and applies the rule directly.

01:21.256 --> 01:27.801
Each vision-language model, or VLM, receives the image and a three-option question that states the rule.

01:27.801 --> 01:35.353
The states are randomly assigned to A, B, and C, and any answer other than one letter is invalid, with the maximum cost.

01:35.353 --> 01:41.898
Supplying no metrics, or the two deciding metrics through prefix or fusion, gives 27 VLM configurations.

01:44.100 --> 01:46.524
Trivial baselines set the floor.

01:46.524 --> 01:52.282
Always answering healthy gives 28.6 percent accuracy, at a mean cost of 5.6.

01:52.282 --> 01:57.509
Always answering degraded gives 40.8 percent, at a cost of only 0.88.

01:59.700 --> 02:05.075
Segmentation reaches 90.8 percent accuracy, with a mean cost of 0.207.

02:05.075 --> 02:15.517
Among the VLMs, BLIP-2 with fusion is the most accurate, at 41.1 percent, and the only configuration above the always-degraded baseline.

02:15.517 --> 02:17.590
But accuracy is not safety.

02:17.590 --> 02:24.500
Its mean cost is 4.23, while LLaVA, at 36 percent accuracy, has the lowest VLM cost, 2.84.

02:24.500 --> 02:28.262
No VLM costs less than always answering degraded.

02:30.500 --> 02:32.660
The confusion matrices show why.

02:32.660 --> 02:39.546
Segmentation errors stay in neighbouring states, and only one of 160 degraded reefs is called healthy.

02:39.546 --> 02:44.474
BLIP-2 with fusion calls 104 degraded reefs healthy, the costliest error.

02:44.474 --> 02:46.431
LLaVA makes half as many, 52.

02:46.431 --> 02:51.292
The segmentation advantage comes mainly from recognizing degraded reefs.

02:53.500 --> 02:55.145
Why do the VLMs struggle?

02:55.145 --> 02:56.725
The raw answers show it.

02:56.725 --> 03:00.739
Zero-shot LLaVA answers A on every image, in every condition.

03:00.739 --> 03:06.793
With randomized options, this spreads its predictions almost evenly across the three states.

03:06.793 --> 03:10.610
The adapted PaliGemma2 Mix answers healthy on every image.

03:10.610 --> 03:17.849
InternVL2.5 and PaliGemma2-PT give mostly invalid answers, so their few high-cost errors do not mean low risk.

03:20.000 --> 03:22.122
Controlled probes narrowed this down.

03:22.122 --> 03:31.411
LLaVA reads the supplied values correctly and answers single threshold questions correctly, but classifies only one of three examples when the steps are combined.

03:31.411 --> 03:35.368
PaliGemma2 Mix mostly picks whichever state the prompt defines first.

03:35.368 --> 03:41.618
On the real images, putting the degraded rule first changed every zero-shot PaliGemma prediction to degraded.

03:43.800 --> 03:45.419
Would more context help?

03:45.419 --> 03:49.938
Supplying all five metrics instead of two is again model-dependent.

03:49.938 --> 03:59.247
For InternVL2.5 with low-rank adaptation and prefix delivery, accuracy rises from 31.6 to 50.3 percent, and invalid outputs roughly halve.

03:59.247 --> 04:02.080
BLIP-2 gets worse, and LLaVA is unchanged.

04:02.080 --> 04:06.465
Strict and relaxed answer parsing give identical primary results.

04:08.700 --> 04:17.272
The cost weights are also operational choices, so we tested 40 cost specifications: 38 strictly ordered matrices and two non-ordered controls.

04:17.272 --> 04:22.101
Segmentation stays lowest-cost in every one, and the model rankings stay stable.

04:24.300 --> 04:27.923
How much measurement error can the segmentation decision tolerate?

04:27.923 --> 04:33.743
We pushed each degraded reef toward healthy, raising live coral and lowering bleaching by the same amount.

04:33.743 --> 04:41.538
The smallest shift that turns a degraded reef healthy is 20.08 percentage points, close to the 20-point gap between the live coral thresholds.

04:41.538 --> 04:46.534
This exceeds the 95th-percentile segmentation errors, but the largest errors can exceed it.

04:48.700 --> 04:50.908
Decision margins give a second view.

04:50.908 --> 04:57.716
Correct segmentation decisions sit a median of 8.3 points from a state boundary, and errors only 3 points away.

04:57.716 --> 05:05.198
But degraded-state errors range up to 66 points from a boundary, so boundary proximity explains only part of the failures.

05:07.400 --> 05:08.609
Three caveats apply.

05:08.609 --> 05:15.561
The reference labels and the segmentation decision use the same measurements, so part of its advantage is expected.

05:15.561 --> 05:20.882
The thresholds and costs are author-defined, and should be calibrated with reef experts.

05:20.882 --> 05:25.960
And one observed high-cost error does not mean such errors stay rare on other reefs.

05:25.960 --> 05:33.154
On a server GPU, the segmentation decision takes 79 milliseconds per image, and the full pipeline with LLaVA takes 166.

05:35.400 --> 05:43.377
In conclusion, an explicit rule on segmentation metrics gives 90.8 percent accuracy and the lowest cost across all tested cost settings.

05:43.377 --> 05:50.416
The most accurate VLM was not the safest, and none reliably applied the rule, even when given the deciding measurements.

05:50.416 --> 05:55.872
Use the language model for flexible questions, and explicit logic for rule-defined decisions.

05:58.300 --> 05:59.324
Thank you for watching.
